   "text": {
    "L1": "Indian farming has a very long story, from early settled farmers to the challenges farmers face today.",
    "L2": "A common belief says ancient Indian farmers had no irrigation or technology. That is not true.",
    "L3": "At Mehrgarh, around 7000 BCE, people grew wheat and barley and kept animals.",
    "L4": "Harappan farmers grew wheat, barley and cotton, and used ploughs. Ancient people also built wells, tanks, stepwells and canals.",
    "L5": "Crops follow seasons: kharif crops are sown with the monsoon, while rabi crops are sown in the cooler months. Rice is kharif, and wheat is rabi.",
    "L6": "The Green Revolution of the 1960s used high-yielding seeds, irrigation and fertilisers, sharply raising grain production, especially wheat and rice.",
    "L7": "Farmers now face small landholdings, uncertain monsoons, falling groundwater, damaged soil, price swings and climate change. Water-saving methods and crop diversity can help.",
    "L8": "The timeline is Mehrgarh, Harappan cotton, Kallanai, then the Green Revolution. Put these farming milestones in order."
   },
   "narration": {
